A||||4||GT Bastard||Anybody mess around with pinhole cameras?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-24-2001||12:36 AM||||If so, got any tips?  My bro is a photographer and his usually turn out pretty interesting.  My first ones are ok, but aren't very exciting.  I'm still experimenting with different exposure times and lighting.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shaft||02-24-2001||12:45 AM||||I've experimented with pinhole camara's in photography class before. They're cool but we never really shent much time on them. They're hard to do a lot of photography tricks or effects with lighting or exposure time. I've found that they work best with objects that are a medium range away, and make sure you keep the pin hole small and round.(better focus) The coolest things that you could probably do with them could be done in the dusk/dark with a flashlight. In low light let the camara sit for a while aimed at dimly lit area...then have someone walk into the area and put a flashlight on only part of their body for a few seconds. If the lighting and exposure times are right you'll just see floating parts of a body in the setting.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-24-2001||09:17 AM||||I haven't played with one since the early 70's, but my advise to you is use your imagination. Try using different lighting and angles. Take dozens and dozens of pics. One might accidently come out exceptional...LOL. It's happened to me before. Take lots of shots of everything!<P>You can also "bracket" your pics...that means trying several different exposure times for the same pic. This increases your odds that one of them will have the right exposure.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick's Dad||02-25-2001||01:42 PM||||I concur with Shaft. The painting with light aspect of pinhole is quite fun. I prefer to use a flash (Like a Vivitar 285) instead of a flashlight, because it takes less exposure time, and you can do multiple "exposures" more easily.<P>I have two pinhole 4x5 cameras, of 37mm and 150mm, and am going to construct a 1000mm pinhole 4x5. Why 4x5? If you can get a graflok back, you can use Polaroid film. I'll be using Type 55 a lot this summer, because my darkroom should be complete by then, and you can use Sodium Sulfite to clear out the T55 negative to use it for contact and enlargment printing.<P>I may try Pinhole Image Transfers, too (Type 59).<P>I also got a 120 format "Zone Plate" camera (http://www.zeroimage.com) last year, and am still experimenting with it. As before, the completed darkroom in my basement will accelerate this work considerably.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/StarHeroSig.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||Spyder||02-27-2001||12:40 AM||||GT Bastard<BR>I thought you were talking about a different kind of pinhole camera.lol||||reg||1
